{"title":"1974 USAMO Problems/Problem 4","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>There is a possible flaw in the original statement. There exists a scenario under which the best strategy would be different.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#TL;DR\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">TL;DR</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1974_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A father, mother and son hold a family tournament, playing a two person board game with no ties. The tournament rules are:\n</p><p>(i) The weakest player chooses the first two contestants.\n</p><p>(ii) The winner of any game plays the next game against the person left out.\n</p><p>(iii) The first person to win two games wins the tournament.\n</p><p>The father is the weakest player, the son the strongest, and it is assumed that any player's probability of winning an individual game from another player does not change during the tournament. Prove that the father's optimal strategy for winning the tournament is to play the first game with his wife.\n</p><p>Note: there is a possible flaw in the original statement. There exists a scenario under which the winning strategy might differ. See my post under AoPS Olympiad Resources/Contest Collections/…/USAMO 1974, where details are shown.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1974_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>There are three possible strategies for the father:\n</p><p>1)  Sit out the first game\n</p><p>2)  Play the first game against the weaker of the other two players (the mother).  This person is referred to throughout the solution as \"the weaker player\".\n</p><p>3)  Play the first game against the stronger of the other two players (the son).  This person is referred to throughout the solution as \"the stronger player\".\n</p><p><br />\nStrategy 1 is dominated by both strategy 2 and strategy 3.  If strategy 1 is used and the weaker player wins the first game, it becomes strategy 2, but with the father starting behind 0-1.  Likewise, if strategy 1 is used and the stronger player wins the first game, it becomes strategy 3, but with the father again starting behind 0-1.\n</p><p><br />\nThus, the father must play the first game.\n</p><p><br />\nBy the pigeonhole principle, there may be no more than 4 games; once the 4th game is finished, someone will have won two games.  Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/c/10cb764f88509fb1c8012366993fdbee98f31bc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"22\" height=\"14\" /> denote a game the father wins, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/9/859ccf4cd60c7bc6b8fa1afc9a42dc811a826d6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> denote a game the father loses, and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> denote a game the father does not participate in.  Then, with the knowledge that the father plays the first game, the following ways for the father to win two games are possible:\n</p><p>(A)  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/e/59e9de4daad35eb3d95fa7eecdc7807dc411e7fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$WW$\" width=\"39\" height=\"12\" />\n</p><p>(B)  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/a/72ab632b3d928bb8c1a32b0687cc36aa58b63aa5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$WLxW$\" width=\"62\" height=\"12\" />\n</p><p>(C)  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/0/a109dd8ce325f95851676cfcbc7736449ec36889.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$LxWW$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"64\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>If the father wins the first game, then he must either win the second game (A), or sit out the third game to win the fourth game (B).\nIf the father loses the first game, then he must sit out the second game and win the third and fourth games (C).\n</p><p>Now we must determine which gives the largest probability of the father winning: playing the first game against the weaker player or the stronger player.  We define the following probabilities:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is the chance of the father defeating the weaker player.  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/e/ebe4bf9c2b96ee54aa7d3e792caf208b6619a5fd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1-x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"54\" height=\"18\" /> is the chance of the weaker player defeating the father.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is the chance of the father defeating the stronger player.  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/5/df50044a9664aa825a551d8b4cbf49d89fff0004.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1-y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"18\" /> is the chance of the stronger player defeating the father.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is the chance of the weaker player defeating the stronger player.  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/2/bd2e06b82b1b2479bbb51d901ee4124a5742c24c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1-z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"18\" /> is the chance of the stronger player defeating the weaker player.\n</p><p>Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/7/ab7da572d60053bdb3ab92f2dda44f87cd9dc78d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x &gt; y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" />, and all of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/5/32581661cddf01ad30c1e36efe72f99eb0a0adca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x, y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"27\" height=\"11\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> are less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/2/192b4a258ee998961a99523fd0f0a029f9ea194a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.5$\" width=\"22\" height=\"12\" />.\n</p><p>If the father plays the weaker player first, we split the probability of winning the tournament in the three mutually exclusive cases above:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/3/c538d37407c94d4fc776ef252b1b661d736a3516.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(A) = xy$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"85\" height=\"18\" /> because the father must defeat the weaker player, then the father must defeat the stronger player.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/1/aa13686eb8503938763b2b32b6ca72355aa2970d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(B) = x(1-y)zx$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"154\" height=\"20\" /> because the father must defeat the weaker player, then stronger player must defeat the father.  Third, the weaker player must defeat the stronger player (because if the stronger player wins a second game, the tournament is over).  Fourth, the father plays the weaker player a second time and must win.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/a/5ca39516cd1b198acb524e3f21cbe01e273f6d70.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(C) = (1-x)(1-z)yx$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"197\" height=\"18\" /> because the weaker player must defeat the father, followed by the stronger player defeating the weaker player (because if the weaker player wins two in a row, he wins the tournament).  The father must then defeat the stronger player and the weaker player in the third and fourth game, respectively.\n</p><p>From this, the total probability of the father winning by playing the weaker player first is:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/9/c89fe34ba789c058bdb780503d28e9d6d4169a3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(TOTAL) = P(A) + P(B) + P(C) = xy + x(1-y)zx + (1-x)(1-z)yx$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"595\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Likewise, we compute the probability of the father winning if he plays the stronger player first.  We do this by symmetry:\n</p><p>All <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> will turn into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, because whenever the father defeated the weaker player before, he must defeat the stronger player now.\n</p><p>All <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> will turn into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, because whenever the father defeated the stronger player before, he must defeat the weaker player now.\n</p><p>All <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> will turn into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/2/bd2e06b82b1b2479bbb51d901ee4124a5742c24c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1-z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"18\" />, because whenever the weaker player defeated the stronger player, now the stronger player must defeat the weaker player.\n</p><p>All <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/2/bd2e06b82b1b2479bbb51d901ee4124a5742c24c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1-z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"18\" /> will turn into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, because whenever the stronger player defeated the weaker player, now the weaker player must defeat the stronger player.\n</p><p>Applying these transformations and calling the new probabilities <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/4/3545551b3d62c4e23d5df7352a55602d0aa9adcd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P&#39;$\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, we find that:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/f/f1fb767355879667836ea46ef67532b196c0e7db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P&#39;(TOTAL) = P&#39;(A) + P&#39;(B) + P&#39;(C) = yx + y(1-x)(1-z)y + (1-y)zxy$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"612\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>We compare these probabilities.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/a/e5a2797e77b192206e5f049260a6a787c3824118.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(A) = P&#39;(A)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"111\" height=\"18\" />.  This makes sense, as the probabilitiy of the father winning the first two games is the same, regardless of the order in which they are played.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/9/c79194571c9b068a5643a8b69d5b66122a084e6b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(B) &gt; P&#39;(C)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"113\" height=\"18\" />:  Calculating <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/4/cd49cc0cb539d90c9f1ab7a0e97d294ead3ee7e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(B) / P&#39;(C)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"98\" height=\"18\" />, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/9/e3935d9470b967b8d9c70638049b3d1728dc5f68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2(1-y)z / xy(1-y)z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"174\" height=\"19\" />, which simplifies to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/6/a16f474c2f9c02c3b39ddd68e96851187b12c4b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x/y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"31\" height=\"20\" />.  \n</p><p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/7/ab7da572d60053bdb3ab92f2dda44f87cd9dc78d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x &gt; y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/3/3b382ff4aa21377d9f45c8246cb49595db1615ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x/y &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/5/265b94cc01529469f815ecc7bf1adc1e6cb4f3d4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(B) / P&#39;(C) &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"131\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/9/c79194571c9b068a5643a8b69d5b66122a084e6b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(B) &gt; P&#39;(C)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"113\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/2/1c2d3282cdeb61951deb3a8f3aa3296b6a06e74f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(C) &gt; P&#39;(B)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"113\" height=\"18\" />:  Calculating <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/2/ff2b1e40d72d9624243b81a345af1ca3a08ea913.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(C) / P&#39;(B)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"98\" height=\"18\" />, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/1/361e0853e22990a96b60c23c31016d93da00e9fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1-x)(1-z)yx / (1-x)(1-z)y^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"265\" height=\"19\" />, which simpllifies again to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/6/a16f474c2f9c02c3b39ddd68e96851187b12c4b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x/y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"31\" height=\"20\" />.  Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/2/1c2d3282cdeb61951deb3a8f3aa3296b6a06e74f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(C) &gt; P&#39;(B)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"113\" height=\"18\" />  \n</p><p>Adding the above equations and inequalities, we see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/f/6ff7d7ea467e37136ec67850a08bedaf984e6d9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(TOTAL) &gt; P&#39;(TOTAL)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"217\" height=\"18\" />, and thus the father is better off playing the weaker of the two players (ie the mother) first.\n</p>\n<h3><span id=\"TL.3BDR\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"TL;DR\">TL;DR</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1974_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: TL;DR\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Let the probability of the father winning against the son be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, the father winning against the mother be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />, and the son winning against the mother be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" />. Here <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/d/f1de8de630d8a84dcbdd5d58fb05c7f3d11f43a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a&lt;b&lt;\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"81\" height=\"38\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/c/46ccab80937a2a6921e82c519793927291ab9c0e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c&gt;\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"47\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>We define three first game cases as above:\n</p><p>1. Father vs Mother\n</p><p>2. Father vs Son\n</p><p>3. Mother vs Son\n</p><p>The probability that the father wins (1) (by casework or tree) is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/6/a96f1b3d136f8da67a6798baf5909680e2411f32.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(1)=ab+b^2(1-a)(1-c)+(1-b)abc$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"328\" height=\"21\" />.\n</p><p>The probability that the father wins (2) is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/9/7898061569ee5924541fd109bae64f699174d0c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(2)=ab+a^2c(1-b)+ab(1-a)(1-c)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"329\" height=\"21\" />.\n</p><p>The probability that the father wins (3) is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/9/ad9e19a6f271f8f46c52fc89375517eedeaef50a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(3)=ab$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"81\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>Evidently <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/6/db6f285fbaafc0de7cefb20b80fb09a48363e8d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(1)&gt;P(3)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"101\" height=\"20\" />, so all that is left is to prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/2/8f2198035647f5852e68aeadbe5f1cb9d701ff30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(1)&gt;P(2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"101\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/2/8f2198035647f5852e68aeadbe5f1cb9d701ff30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(1)&gt;P(2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"101\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/3/d73ffbf4fc029447db7bb5bcca0538913a62f406.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ab+b^2(1-a)(1-c)+(1-b)abc&gt;ab+a^2c(1-b)+ab(1-a)(1-c)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"556\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/6/cc65bb1370cc4f7e7aeb0306d1109d4d75ea00b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b^2(1-a)(1-c)+(1-b)abc&gt;a^2c(1-b)+ab(1-a)(1-c)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"477\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/f/35fc5ddfbd0d4e0cb708340865f4bf83b367fddb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b(b-a)(1-a)(1-c)&gt;ac(a-b)(1-b)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"321\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>Using the fact that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/0/710e3e4f18d7c0270ab719a00adc61fa12363b1e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a&lt;b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" />, we easily find that the LHS is positive while the RHS is negative, proving the inequality.\n</p><p>~eevee9406\n</p><p><i>Alternate solutions are always welcome. If you have a different, elegant solution to this problem, please <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=1974_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit\">add it</a> to this page.</i>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1974_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1974_USAMO\" title=\"1974 USAMO\">1974 USAMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1974_USAMO_Problems\" title=\"1974 USAMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=27&amp;year=1974\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1974_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1974 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1974_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1974 USAMO Problems/Problem 5\">Problem 5</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1974_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1974 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1974_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1974 USAMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1974_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1974 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1974_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1974 USAMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017102615\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.046 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.072 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 353/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1617/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 44/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 10906/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   23.554      1 -total\n 37.51%    8.836      1 Template:USAMO_box\n 31.22%    7.354      1 Template:Alternate_solutions\n 30.18%    7.109      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_